+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 global fetch() honour HTTPS_PROXY by default?
+
+## Answer
+
+**No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTPS_PROXY`, `HTTP_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` by default.
+Node only reads these variables if you turn proxy support on:
+
+- set `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`, or
+- start Node with `--use-env-proxy`.
+
+Both options were **added in v22.21.0**. Node marks them *Stability 1.1 – Active Development*.
+On Node 22 releases before 22.21.0, there is no built-in way to make `fetch()` use the environment proxy.
+You have to set an undici `EnvHttpProxyAgent` or `ProxyAgent` yourself with `setGlobalDispatcher`, using the `undici` npm package.
+
+## Evidence
+
+### 1. Node's v22 documentation
+
+From the CLI docs, `--use-env-proxy` and `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` (https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html):
+
+> Added in: v22.21.0 — Stability: 1.1 - Active Development
+>
+> When enabled, Node.js parses the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables
+> during startup, and tunnels requests over the specified proxy.
+>
+> This is equivalent to setting the `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` environment variable. When both are set,
+> `--use-env-proxy` takes precedence.
+
+From the HTTP docs, "Built-in Proxy Support" (https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/http.html#built-in-proxy-support), added in v22.21.0:
+
+> When Node.js creates the global agent, if the `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` environment variable is set to `1`
+> or `--use-env-proxy` is enabled, the global agent will be constructed with `proxyEnv: process.env`,
+> enabling proxy support based on the environment variables.
+
+The same section explains how the variables are read:
+- Lowercase names win over uppercase ones (`https_proxy` beats `HTTPS_PROXY`).
+- `NO_PROXY` holds the hosts that skip the proxy.
+- Requests to Unix domain sockets ignore the proxy.
+
+v22.21.0 also added a `proxyEnv` option to `http.Agent`, for custom agents.
+
+The documentation says "when enabled", so the behaviour is opt-in. One caveat: neither docs page names `fetch()` explicitly. The HTTP section only covers `http`/`https` agents. That `fetch()` is covered comes from the test below.
+
+### 2. Test on the local Node build
+
+The test script is at `/tmp/claude-0/-tmp-hs-bench-sgr7jA/8e2ddea9-627b-4ed9-a2e9-c8d60a8fb338/scratchpad/proxytest.js`. It works like this:
+- It starts a fake proxy on 127.0.0.1.
+- It sets `HTTPS_PROXY` to point at that proxy.
+- It runs `fetch('https://example.invalid/')` in a child process under each setting and counts how many requests reach the proxy.
+
+Result on Node **v22.23.4** (bundled undici 6.29.1):
+
+| Setting | Result | Requests that reached the proxy |
+|---|---|---|
+| `HTTPS_PROXY` only (default) | `ENOTFOUND`: fetch connected directly and DNS failed | **0** |
+| `HTTPS_PROXY` + `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` | went through the proxy (the child printed a runtime warning) | **1** |
+| `HTTPS_PROXY` + `--use-env-proxy` | went through the proxy (the child printed a runtime warning) | **1** |
+
+So in Node 22.21.0 and later, the switch applies to global `fetch()` as well as `http`/`https`. Without the switch, `fetch()` ignores the variables.
+
+## Practical guidance
+
+- On Node ≥ 22.21.0, run with `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` or `--use-env-proxy`. You can also put the flag in `NODE_OPTIONS`.
+- On Node < 22.21.0, install `undici` and call
+  `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())` early in startup.
+- The feature is still marked Active Development. The details may change in later 22.x releases.
+
